2026-10-20|Lexicographically Largest Array Using Adjacent Swaps

Question:
Given an array arr[], you may swap adjacent elements arr[i] and arr[i + 1] only if their sum is even. Find the lexicographically largest array obtainable by performing this operation any number of times, including zero times.

Approach:
1. Traverse the array and identify consecutive groups of elements with the same parity.
2. Within each group, collect the elements.
3. Sort the elements of each group in descending order.
4. Place the sorted elements back into their original group positions.
5. Return the modified array.

Why this works:
- Two adjacent elements can be swapped exactly when they have the same parity.
- Elements of different parity cannot cross each other.
- Consequently, each element must remain within its original consecutive parity group.
- Within a group, any permutation is possible through adjacent swaps, so sorting the group in descending order gives the lexicographically largest arrangement.

Solution:
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> lexicographicallyLargest(vector<int>& arr) {
        int n = arr.size();
        int i = 0;

        while (i < n) {
            int j = i;

            while (j + 1 < n &&
                   abs(arr[j] % 2) == abs(arr[j + 1] % 2)) {
                j++;
            }

            sort(arr.begin() + i, arr.begin() + j + 1, greater<int>());

            i = j + 1;
        }

        return arr;
    }
};
```

Example:

Input: arr = [1, 5, 3, 2, 4, 6]

The consecutive parity groups are:
- [1, 5, 3] — all odd.
- [2, 4, 6] — all even.

After sorting each group in descending order:

Output: [5, 3, 1, 6, 4, 2]

Time Complexity: O(n log n) in the worst case, due to sorting the parity groups.

Space Complexity: O(log n) auxiliary space for sorting, excluding the input array.
